{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/memory","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Memory","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Distinguish parametric, working, and long-term memory","Explain why working memory is just the context window","Explain why an LLM is stateless during generation, and how a conversation is faked","Describe how long-term memory persists across sessions","Weigh parametric against non-parametric memory, including why only one of them can cite its source"]},"translatedObjectives":null,"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/llms","number":1,"title":"LLM Fundamentals","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},{"key":"fundamentals/rag","number":3,"title":"RAG & Embeddings","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/tools","number":4,"title":"Tool Calling","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/agents","number":6,"title":"AI Agents","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"three-types-of-memory","text":"Three Types of Memory","depth":2},{"id":"parametric-memory","text":"Parametric Memory","depth":2},{"id":"working-memory","text":"Working Memory","depth":2},{"id":"an-llm-is-stateless-during-generation","text":"An LLM is Stateless During Generation","depth":3},{"id":"long-term-memory","text":"Long-term Memory","depth":2},{"id":"side-by-side-comparison","text":"Side-by-side Comparison","depth":2},{"id":"pros-and-cons","text":"Pros and Cons","depth":2},{"id":"key-terms","text":"Key Terms","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3},{"id":"-modules","text":"🧭 Modules","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Before we get to agents, here is one idea that sits quietly under everything so far: an LLM does not remember anything on its own. And \"memory\" is not one thing. There are three kinds. They live in different places, and they behave in completely different ways."}],"lead":true},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"three-types-of-memory","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Three Types of Memory"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (offline, permanent): knowledge that training or fine-tuning stores inside the model's weights."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Short-term memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":", also called "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"working memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (online, temporary): all the text that is inside the LLM's context window right now."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-term memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (online, temporary): a summary or index of past conversations and documents. It is stored outside the model. When it is needed, it is retrieved back into working memory."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Let's go through each one."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parametric-memory","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric Memory"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Remember "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training LLMs"}]},{"type":"text","text":", where we trained and fine-tuned models? Say you fine-tune a model on a large set of legal documents. That information is then stored permanently inside the model's weights (its parameters)."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanent"}]},{"type":"text","text":": it does not disappear when the session ends. You also do not need to send it again on every call. It is just "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"in"}]},{"type":"text","text":" the model."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Imprecise at scale"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the problem is that a model's weights hold a very large amount of information, from billions of documents. When you push your one document in among all of that, it becomes hard for the model to memorize it and retrieve it "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"exactly"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Think of it like a person who has read 1,000 books. They know in general what those books were about. But ask them to quote page 214 of book #537 word for word, and they will struggle. The information is in there somewhere, but they cannot retrieve it precisely."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"working-memory","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Working Memory"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It is useful to call this memory what it really is: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"working memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":". It is the memory that the LLM is actively \"working with\" at this exact moment. That is the text inside its context window right now."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare it to the person-with-1000-books analogy. Working memory is like that same person, but now they have "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"one specific book open right in front of their eyes"}]},{"type":"text","text":". They do not need to recall anything from a vague memory of everything they have ever read. They can just read it. That is why LLMs do so much better with working memory than with parametric memory. The information is not buried among billions of other documents. It is right there in front of them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem"}]},{"type":"text","text":": working memory is limited in size (for example, 200K or 1M tokens), and it can fill up. And when you start a new session (a new Claude Code or ChatGPT conversation), it is gone. Every new session starts with a completely empty context window. This is because the LLM keeps no state of its own between sessions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"an-llm-is-stateless-during-generation","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An LLM is Stateless During Generation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is an important distinction: LLMs are stateless "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"during generation"}]},{"type":"text","text":". (Training is different. Training is a one-time process, and it produces parametric memory.) \"During generation\" means every time the model replies to you. During that time, the model saves nothing on its own. Technically, every single message you send is a new and independent call to the LLM, as if it were a new session. This is because the LLM itself keeps no state of the conversation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"So why does it feel like a continuous conversation? Because "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"we"}]},{"type":"text","text":" fake it. We keep a growing stack of every message exchanged so far. Something new happens each time you send a message or the LLM generates a reply. Every time, we add that new message to the stack. Then, on the next call, we send the "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"entire stack"}]},{"type":"text","text":" back to the LLM."}]},{"type":"image","number":"5.1","title":"The stack that is working memory","note":"This stack is working memory. You write a message, and the model writes one back. Nothing is ever removed. On the second turn, the model reads everything from the first turn again. This is because we send this whole container on every single call.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/llm-context.webp","alt":"The stack that is working memory","width":1600,"height":894},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice that every call sends the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"whole"}]},{"type":"text","text":" stack, not only the newest message. This is because the LLM remembers nothing from the previous call on its own. So \"memory\" here is really only this: we show it everything again, every single time."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"5.2","title":"An LLM is Stateless During Generation","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/97a21945b3f9cdab81f94ab5a8a62adf.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/d3af74a02e3c1661994ca15d7a4a3b3d.svg"}},{"type":"image","number":"5.3","title":"Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory","note":"Short-term (working) memory is just this growing stack of Human and AI messages. Long-term memory is a separate store. The stack is saved into this store and retrieved from it, across sessions. The labels \"Checkpointer\" and \"Store\" come from the LangGraph framework. Other frameworks use different names for the same idea.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/short-vs-long-memory.webp","alt":"Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory","width":1142,"height":744},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"long-term-memory","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-term Memory"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"We go much deeper into this later, in the Expert track's "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"expert/advanced-memory","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" module. For now, here is the basic idea."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Sometimes we do not let working memory disappear when a session ends. Instead, we save a summary or an index of it somewhere outside the model. Later, in a completely different session, we can pull that saved information back into working memory when it is needed. We often use "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to do this."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Example"}]},{"type":"text","text":": say you upload 5 PDFs to ChatGPT in one session. Later, you start a brand-new session and ask a question about those PDFs. ChatGPT may still answer. This is not because the model \"remembers\" them in its weights (parametric memory). It is also not because they are in the new session's context window (working memory), which is empty. It is because ChatGPT indexed those PDFs during the earlier session. Now that you ask about them again, it can retrieve the relevant parts back into context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"side-by-side-comparison","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Side-by-side Comparison"}]},{"type":"table","number":"5.1","title":"Side-by-side Comparison","rowHeaders":true,"align":[null,null,null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Working (Short-Term)"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-Term"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Stored in"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Model weights"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context window"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"External storage (DB, vector store, files)"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Persistence"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanent"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Temporary, gone when the session ends or the context fills up"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Persists across sessions"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Precision"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fuzzy: hard to recall exactly among billions of documents"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Very precise: the LLM reads it directly"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Precise once retrieved back into context"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Created by"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training / fine-tuning"}]}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A growing stack of messages"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit save + retrieval, often with "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG"}]}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Analogy"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Someone who has read 1,000 books"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Someone reading a book open right in front of them"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Someone's own notes, looked up later"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The main point: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"the LLM itself has no memory of its own. Parametric memory is what training stored in its weights. Working memory is what your app is showing it right now. Long-term memory is what your app saves and brings back later."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pros-and-cons","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pros and Cons"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Working memory and long-term memory have one thing in common. In both, the information sits outside the weights, as text that the model reads. So we group the two under one name: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"non-parametric memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":". The name comes from the "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"original RAG paper"}]},{"type":"text","text":", which used it for knowledge kept outside the weights. Here is how the two families compare."}]},{"type":"table","number":"5.2","title":"Pros and Cons","rowHeaders":true,"align":[null,null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric (in the weights)"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Non-parametric (in the context, or retrieved into it)"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost on each call"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"No extra tokens: the knowledge is already inside the model"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You pay for every token you put into the context, on every call"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Changing it"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You have to train again, which takes hours or days and costs real money"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You edit a file or a database row, and the next call sees the change"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Freshness"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Frozen at the moment training ended"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Live: as current as the data you store"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Where an answer came from"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unknown: you cannot see which training data produced it"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Known: you can point at the exact document the model read"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Checking an answer"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hard, because there is nothing to compare it with"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Easier: compare the answer with the text the model was shown"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Good for"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"How the model behaves: a style, a format, a skill"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What the model should know: facts, documents, data that changes"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The row that matters most is the source. When a model answers from its weights, you usually cannot tell which training data it is using. You also cannot tell whether that data was right. The knowledge is frozen and has no address. Go back to the person who has read 1,000 books. They can tell you a fact, but they cannot tell you which book it came from. Now give them their own notes. They can point at the page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A non-parametric answer can be "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"cited"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the app can show the user the document the model had in front of it. This is one of the main reasons why "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":" usually beats fine-tuning. It is the better way to give a model your own documents."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"key-terms","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Key Terms"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the knowledge that training stores in the model's weights, which is permanent but imprecise at scale."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":": training a model on a set of documents, so that the information stays in its weights."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Working memory (short-term memory)"}]},{"type":"text","text":": all the text inside the context window at this moment."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context window"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the space that holds working memory, which has a limited size, for example 200K or 1M tokens."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-term memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a summary or index of past conversations and documents, stored outside the model and retrieved when needed."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Stateless"}]},{"type":"text","text":": during generation, the model keeps no state, so each call is new and independent."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Message stack"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the growing stack of every message exchanged so far, which the app sends in full on each call."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the method that retrieves stored text back into the context, often for long-term memory."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Non-parametric memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":": knowledge kept as text outside the weights, and both working memory and long-term memory belong to it."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Citation"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the source document that an answer points to, which only non-parametric memory can provide."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"There are three kinds of memory, and you cannot use one in place of another. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parametric memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is permanent, and training puts it into the weights. But it is fuzzy, because your data is mixed in with billions of other documents. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Working memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is precise but temporary: it is just a growing stack of messages that we send again on every call. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-term memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is saved outside the model and retrieved back into working memory when needed, often with RAG. Only the memory outside the weights can show where an answer came from. Next come agents. They depend on this same working memory to plan and act across many steps."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the original RAG paper, which named parametric and non-parametric memory"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://medium.com/@sahilnanga4/the-three-memory-types-every-llm-developer-must-know-3358c26fdff3"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The three memory types every LLM developer must know"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the same split, seen from a different angle"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-modules","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🧭 Modules"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG & Embeddings"}]},{"type":"text","text":": how long-term memory gets retrieved back into working memory"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"expert/advanced-memory","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":": where this goes next"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"What is working memory?","options":["Knowledge that training stores in the model's weights","A summary of past sessions in an external database","The text that is inside the context window right now","A record of the chat that the model keeps by itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"Working memory is the text in the context window at this moment. The model does not keep it by itself, so it is gone when a new session starts."},{"question":"Parametric memory is permanent. Why is it still imprecise?","options":["The model deletes it when the session ends","Your document is mixed with billions of others in the weights","The context window is too small to hold all of it","It is stored in a database outside the model"],"answer":1,"explain":"The weights hold information from billions of documents, so the model cannot recall one document exactly. Parametric memory does not disappear when a session ends."},{"question":"An LLM is stateless during generation. How does a chat still feel like one continuous conversation?","options":["The model saves each new message in its weights","The model remembers the previous call by itself","The app sends only the newest message to the model","The app sends the whole message stack on every call"],"answer":3,"explain":"The app keeps a growing stack of all messages and sends the whole stack on every call. The model remembers nothing from the previous call, so the newest message alone is not enough."},{"question":"You upload 5 PDFs to ChatGPT. Later, in a new session, it answers a question about them. How can it do this?","options":["ChatGPT indexed the PDFs and retrieves the relevant parts into context","The upload stored the PDFs permanently in the model's weights","The PDFs are still in the new session's context window","The LLM keeps the earlier session in its own state"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is long-term memory: the PDFs were indexed outside the model and retrieved back into context, often with RAG. A new session starts with an empty context window, so working memory cannot explain it."},{"question":"Why can an answer from non-parametric memory be cited, when one from the weights cannot?","options":["Because the weights are encrypted after training ends","Because non-parametric memory is always more accurate","Because a model forgets its training data after each call","Because you know exactly which document the model read"],"answer":3,"explain":"Retrieved text sits in the context, so the app can show which document the answer came from. Knowledge in the weights has no address, so it is very hard to say which training data produced it."}]}